{
  "core_message": "Sickle‑cell disease turns red blood cells into rigid sickles that block blood flow, causing pain and organ damage.",
  "mood": "curious",
  "title": "5 Years of Pain: The Day Sickle Cells Became Rigid Clogs",
  "description": "Sickle‑cell disease changes the shape of red blood cells, turning them into stiff sickles that clog tiny vessels. The blockage sparks severe pain, organ failure, and early death unless treated with blood transfusions, medication, and the newest gene therapies. This short explains how a single genetic mutation leads to a global health crisis.\n\nKey facts:\n- 7.7 million affected worldwide\n- 34 000 deaths annually\n- Gene therapy approved in 2023\n\nBased on the Wikipedia article \"Sickle cell disease\" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sickle_cell_disease), licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.\n\nImages from Wikimedia Commons:\nThe diseases of infancy and childhood (1920) (14768428942).jpg (Internet Archive Book Images, No restrictions): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_diseases_of_infancy_and_childhood_(1920)_(14768428942).jpg\nSickle cells.jpg (Dr Graham Beards, CC BY-SA 3.0): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sickle_cells.jpg\nSchematic of alkaline hemoglobin electrophoresis.png (Spicy, CC BY-SA 4.0): https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schematic_of_alkaline_hemoglobin_electrophoresis.png\n\n3D models:\nHuman Hemoglobin Molecular Structure by S.Duce (CC BY 4.0) https://sketchfab.com/3d-models/none-70169635dec246a6af4db09f2abf68f6",
